{
  "summary": "The round-3 proposal to use os.execv or app.exit() for a full TUI process restart has several unaddressed gaps: (1) os.execv(sys.executable, ...) must preserve sys.argv to correctly relaunch with the same arguments/board/config, but no proposal mentions this — a bare os.execv(sys.executable, [sys.executable]) would restart Python with no arguments, losing the user's session context entirely. (2) app.exit() alone does NOT restart anything — it just terminates the Textual app; there is no auto-relaunch wrapper or supervisor mentioned, so calling app.exit() from the webui would simply kill the TUI with no recovery, which is worse than the current no-op behavior. If app.exit() is the approach, the TUI's entry point (the script that calls app.run()) needs a restart loop that detects a restart exit code vs. a normal exit, and this architectural change hasn't been scoped. (3) The kill logic only addresses _plan_agent_status and _implement_agent_status but ignores _script_status — if a done-script is running when restart is triggered, it would be orphaned or killed uncleanly. (4) For the modal conversion: client.html has .modal-overlay infrastructure but lacks a showToast() function, while index.html has showToast but uses it inconsistently. The standardization proposal ('modal for confirmation, showToast for success') requires adding showToast to client.html as well, which should be explicitly noted. (5) A simpler and more robust alternative to os.execv that nobody has proposed: have _handle_remote_restart kill all running agents, clear state, and call the existing app refresh/reconnect logic — effectively a 'soft restart' that reloads features and re-initializes the display without killing the process. This avoids the os.execv argument-preservation problem and the app.exit supervisor-loop problem, and is more aligned with what the user likely means by 'restart' (refresh stale state) rather than a literal process restart."
}
